        "title": "Computing the Tangent Line to a Parametric Curve"
      },
      "question": {
        ":question/id": "fe828f0d-b796-5138-8bc4-7649d4f442dc",
        ":question/problem": "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=-3 t+5,\\qquad y=2 t^{3}+t,\\qquad -\\infty<t<\\infty.\n$$\n\nCalculate the equation of the tangent to the curve at the point where $t=1.$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "First, we find the coordinates of the point by substituting $t=1$ into the parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =-3 (1)+5=-3+5=2 \\\\ y & =2 (1)^{3}+(1)=2+1=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we're looking to find the equation of the tangent line to the curve at the point $(2,3).$\n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we need to find $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}.$ To compute it, we first need to find the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\nx'(t)=-3,\\qquad y'(t)=6 t^{2}+1.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}=\\frac{y'(t)}{x'(t)}=\\frac{6 t^{2}+1}{-3}.\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=1,$ the slope of the tangent line is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{6 (1)^{2}+1}{-3}=\\frac{7}{-3}=-\\frac{7}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to calculate the equation of the tangent line, we use point-slope form with the point $(x_1,y_1)=(2,3)$ and the slope $m=-\\frac{7}{3}.$ We get\n\n$$\ny-3=-\\frac{7}{3}(x-2).\n$$",
        ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
